  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report on the Power Marketing Administrations (PMAs)**\n\nThe Power Marketing Administrations (PMAs) are four regional agencies operated by the Department of Energy to market and sell electric power produced by federal dams. The PMAs are: Bonneville Power Administration (BPA), Southeastern Power Administration (SEPA), Southwestern Power Administration (SWPA), and Western Area Power Administration (WAPA). Each PMA operates in a distinct geographic area and has unique elements and regional issues that affect its business.\n\nThe PMAs were created to dispose of excess electric power produced by federal dams and to provide service to small communities and farms that would not be profitable for private utilities to serve. The agencies must give preference to public utility districts and cooperatives, selling power at cost-based rates set at the lowest possible rate consistent with sound business principles.\n\nThe report highlights the financial health and challenges faced by each PMA. BPA, the largest PMA, is self-financed and has a debt of $5.53 billion, which it repays through electricity sales. SEPA, the smallest PMA, has an overcapacity issue and has had to terminate contracts with some customers. SWPA has been challenged by low water conditions, which require it to purchase replacement power and energy to meet its contractual obligations. WAPA, the newest and largest PMA, has been working to address the changing electric power needs of its customers and has implemented a drought-adder reduction program to help smooth annual differences in power generation.\n\nThe report also discusses recent proposals to sell the transmission assets owned and operated by the federal PMAs. The Trump Administration proposed to sell the transmission assets, but the plan was dropped due to stakeholder opposition. The proposal could have had an indirect impact on the original congressional intent for the PMAs to provide electricity at the lowest possible cost.\n\nIn conclusion, the PMAs play a critical role in providing electric power to communities across the country. While each PMA faces unique challenges, they are working to address these issues and provide reliable and affordable power to their customers. The report highlights the importance of the PMAs and the need for continued congressional support to ensure their continued success.",
